#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fault-rig - stage the audit's failure modes so couchd's shadow log can be
graded on the repairs that never fire on their own (C9 INJECTED coverage).

Guard invariants and reconcile repairs are the two responsibilities an evening
of ordinary play never exercises: the console has to actually go wrong first.
The 4 Aug audit staged those states by hand with decoy processes and hand-written
/tmp flags; this is that technique, scripted, logged, and reversible.

DAYTIME JOB, OPERATOR PRESENT. Nothing here runs without --i-am-present, and
every injection arms an auto-restore watchdog BEFORE it touches anything, so a
dropped ssh session, a crashed rig or a forgetful operator all end the same way:
the fault is undone and the old stack comes back on its own.

  tools/fault-rig list
  tools/fault-rig status
  tools/fault-rig inject orphaned-session --i-am-present
  tools/fault-rig restore --i-am-present          # abort, idempotent

What each injection does, in one sentence: put the console into one of the
states the old stack has a repair for, keep the old stack's repair loop out of
the way for a few seconds, and see whether couchd's would-do log says the right
thing first, within its own predicted deadline (C17).

Evidence lands in three places:
  ~/couch/shadow/injections-YYYYMMDD.jsonl   the markers (this file's output)
  ~/couch/shadow/couchd-YYYYMMDD.jsonl       couchd's decisions (the answer)
  /tmp/legacy-intents.jsonl                  the old stack's decisions
and `tools/shadow-diff --since HH:MM:SS` correlates them; every marker prints
the exact command to use.

Read docs/fault-rig-runbook.md before running any of this at the TV.
"""
